WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.963
Welcome back to Building
Global Secure Access.

2
00:00:02.963 --> 00:00:07.600
This is Chapter 8: rings, metrics,
and the evidence package.

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.800
A ring exits on numbers,
not on a quiet week.

4
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.764
This chapter computes the guide's numbers,
says go or no-go, writes the rollback

5
00:00:15.764 --> 00:00:18.401
before it's needed, and hands the security

6
00:00:18.401 --> 00:00:21.038
officer a package an assessor can re-hash.

7
00:00:21.038 --> 00:00:23.552
The chapter is a draft awaiting review,

8
00:00:23.552 --> 00:00:27.440
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

9
00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:32.153
Why numbers?

10
00:00:32.153 --> 00:00:37.046
Because a ring that exits on the calendar
carries its problems to a larger group:

11
00:00:37.046 --> 00:00:40.531
users the client doesn't cover,
policies that would block

12
00:00:40.531 --> 00:00:44.090
people the service carries, a
certificate about to expire.

13
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.526
So the goal: decide each ring's
exit from measured values;

14
00:00:47.526 --> 00:00:51.260
roll back in the guide's order
from a plan a reviewer approved,

15
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.904
written before it's needed;

16
00:00:52.904 --> 00:00:57.609
and give the security officer and the
assessor one evidence folder, with the

17
00:00:57.609 --> 00:01:02.315
controls and indicators each run supports,
where every file can be verified.

18
00:01:02.315 --> 00:01:06.097
It's for the developers, the
program manager who runs the rings,

19
00:01:06.097 --> 00:01:09.360
the security monitor,
and the security officer.

20
00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:17.422
Learn says traffic and remote
network health logs stay thirty days,

21
00:01:17.422 --> 00:01:22.042
names the four tables, and says a sudden
drop in traffic volume is an alert,

22
00:01:22.042 --> 00:01:24.660
because offboarding has no audit signal.

23
00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.315
Its deployment guide asks
for a rollback plan,

24
00:01:27.315 --> 00:01:30.598
and its operations guide for
at least two people per role.

25
00:01:30.598 --> 00:01:36.036
The guide adds the rings, the metrics and
the rollback order, with signaling last.

26
00:01:36.036 --> 00:01:39.214
And CISA's remote user
use case says visibility

27
00:01:39.214 --> 00:01:42.235
must be preserved through
telemetry sharing.

28
00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:46.907
One thing isn't rebuilt: the Zero Trust
Assessment results kit already turns an

29
00:01:46.907 --> 00:01:51.723
assessment into the closure register, and
its network family points at the guide.

30
00:01:51.723 --> 00:01:55.170
This chapter's evidence is
what that register cites.

31
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.083
Here are the numbers.

32
00:02:00.083 --> 00:02:04.216
Eight metrics from one collection
and the inputs: client coverage,

33
00:02:04.216 --> 00:02:08.050
sign-ins through the service,
compliant network enforcement,

34
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:12.560
Private Access least privilege,
connector resilience, bypass exposure,

35
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.070
TLS health, and telemetry freshness,
each with a target from the settings.

36
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:22.144
And eight exit criteria from the guide's
ring table: business days in the ring,

37
00:02:22.144 --> 00:02:27.378
open severity-two tickets, help desk
volume, the client on every in-scope device,

38
00:02:27.378 --> 00:02:31.244
clean report-only results, TLS
failures under one percent,

39
00:02:31.244 --> 00:02:36.720
the emergency exclusion tested on the ring's
start date, and break-glass rehearsed.

40
00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.450
The ring exits only if
every criterion passes.

41
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.675
The rollback plan follows
the guide's order.

42
00:02:46.675 --> 00:02:51.500
Enforced policies go to report-only,
from Step 10 back to Step 5.

43
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.138
Profile links come off,
which is a portal step,

44
00:02:54.138 --> 00:02:57.987
and tagging goes off while the
tenant restrictions policy stays.

45
00:02:57.987 --> 00:03:02.038
The groups come off the profiles
and the VPN route comes back.

46
00:03:02.038 --> 00:03:04.549
Signaling is last, and only on request;

47
00:03:04.549 --> 00:03:09.250
the plan refuses it if a compliant
network policy would stay enforced.

48
00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:12.783
The evidence chain copies
each run with its manifest,

49
00:03:12.783 --> 00:03:18.162
adds its controls and indicators, every
file's hash and the previous record's hash.

50
00:03:18.162 --> 00:03:21.221
A check with a failure
is recorded as findings,

51
00:03:21.221 --> 00:03:24.240
not pass, and doesn't
count toward a stage.

52
00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:28.928
Who does this?

53
00:03:28.928 --> 00:03:32.965
The technical program manager runs
the waves and keeps the ring record.

54
00:03:32.965 --> 00:03:35.865
The service owner approves
each ring's exit.

55
00:03:35.865 --> 00:03:39.031
The security monitor
watches freshness and agrees

56
00:03:39.031 --> 00:03:41.977
the CISA telemetry path
with the TIC delegates.

57
00:03:41.977 --> 00:03:46.127
The security officer keeps the
evidence folder, inside the boundary.

58
00:03:46.127 --> 00:03:50.941
And the assessor or authorizing official
decides each finding's closure:

59
00:03:50.941 --> 00:03:53.466
the kit only says the evidence is ready.

60
00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:56.215
Learn's advice is at
least two people per role.

61
00:03:56.215 --> 00:03:58.891
The reads are Chapter
2's, plus the workspace,

62
00:03:58.891 --> 00:04:03.640
and nothing writes unless a rollback
is applied with its approved hash.

63
00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:08.202
Lab 1.

64
00:04:08.202 --> 00:04:12.203
After collecting the pilot, step
2 runs the telemetry checks.

65
00:04:12.203 --> 00:04:14.145
The setting misses a category;

66
00:04:14.145 --> 00:04:18.576
branch health has no rows because no
branch is on a remote network yet;

67
00:04:18.576 --> 00:04:22.852
alerts are twenty-seven hours old;
and CISA telemetry isn't agreed.

68
00:04:22.852 --> 00:04:26.390
Step 3 computes the metrics
and the pilot's exit.

69
00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:31.469
No metric is met, and the pilot is no-go,
for five reasons: five business days in,

70
00:04:31.469 --> 00:04:36.794
one severity-two ticket open, eighty-three
percent coverage, three report-only

71
00:04:36.794 --> 00:04:41.628
failures carried by the service, and
one and a half percent TLS failures.

72
00:04:41.628 --> 00:04:46.880
The emergency exclusion was tested at
ring start and break-glass was rehearsed.

73
00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:54.065
Step 4 does the same for
ring 1, two weeks later:

74
00:04:54.065 --> 00:04:58.892
every metric is met, every criterion
passes, and the ring says go.

75
00:04:58.892 --> 00:05:02.254
Step 5 plans ring 1's
rollback before it's needed.

76
00:05:02.254 --> 00:05:07.355
Three enforced policies go to
report-only in the guide's order,

77
00:05:07.355 --> 00:05:08.855
Step 7, then 6, then 5.

78
00:05:08.855 --> 00:05:10.117
Tagging goes off.

79
00:05:10.117 --> 00:05:13.964
The profile links and group
assignments are portal steps,

80
00:05:13.964 --> 00:05:16.768
and the VPN route is
the agency's own change.

81
00:05:16.768 --> 00:05:18.131
Signaling stays on.

82
00:05:18.131 --> 00:05:22.750
The plan's hash is printed, and
it's a dry run: nothing changed.

83
00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.150
Step 6 asks for signaling off as well.

84
00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:31.356
The plan adds it as the very last step,

85
00:05:31.356 --> 00:05:36.504
after its own steps have moved the enforced
compliant network policy to report-only;

86
00:05:36.504 --> 00:05:40.475
it would refuse if any compliant
network policy stayed enforced.

87
00:05:40.475 --> 00:05:45.469
Step 7 records the collection, the
metrics and the rollback plan as evidence,

88
00:05:45.469 --> 00:05:48.719
and checks the folder:
every file matches its hash,

89
00:05:48.719 --> 00:05:53.475
the three records form an unbroken
chain, and each record lists its steps.

90
00:05:53.475 --> 00:05:57.830
Step 8 runs the tests:
ten passed, none failed.

91
00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:04.644
Lab 2 uses the test tenant
from Chapters 4 to 7,

92
00:06:04.644 --> 00:06:09.117
with the diagnostic setting on and
at least a day of pilot traffic.

93
00:06:09.117 --> 00:06:10.930
Run the guide's freshness query.

94
00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:15.063
Collect, check telemetry, and
compute the ring's metrics and exit,

95
00:06:15.063 --> 00:06:19.430
using your own ring record from the
template, kept on agency storage.

96
00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:23.492
Plan the rollback and file it with the
change record, without applying it.

97
00:06:23.492 --> 00:06:28.517
Record every run in a protected evidence
folder inside the boundary and check it.

98
00:06:28.517 --> 00:06:30.195
Nothing changed in the tenant,

99
00:06:30.195 --> 00:06:33.080
so there's nothing to back
out but the working copies.

100
00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.654
Then re-run the Zero Trust Assessment and

101
00:06:35.654 --> 00:06:38.455
point each network
finding at this evidence.

102
00:06:38.455 --> 00:06:40.810
The authors haven't run this lab.

103
00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:50.254
Fresh tables and an agreed CISA path
support event logging, audit review and

104
00:06:50.254 --> 00:06:56.660
monitoring, with OMB M-26-14 and TIC
3.0's telemetry requirement behind them.

105
00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:01.685
Ring exits on measured criteria support
change control and impact analysis.

106
00:07:01.685 --> 00:07:05.585
A rollback planned before it's needed
supports the contingency plan.

107
00:07:05.585 --> 00:07:08.885
And the hashed chain supports
protecting audit information.

108
00:07:08.885 --> 00:07:13.610
The assessor or authorizing official
decides the controls, and closure.

109
00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:18.338
The dangers: a ring that exits on the
calendar, the VPN's web path retired

110
00:07:18.338 --> 00:07:23.147
without CISA telemetry, and signaling
turned off first in an incident.

111
00:07:23.147 --> 00:07:26.849
You're done when the tables are
fresh, each ring's exit is filed,

112
00:07:26.849 --> 00:07:29.810
and its rollback was
reviewed before it started.

113
00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:33.760
Next, Chapter 9: deploying
to test, and production.
